{"skill_id":"local-linear-approximation","version":3,"questions":[{"id":"local-linear-approximation-r1-q1","version":1,"round":1,"type":"mc","prompt":"The linear approximation of a smooth scalar function at a chosen point is","options":["the value at that point, plus the derivative there times the step away from it","the value at the new point, with the derivative ignored","the ratio of the function value to the coordinate, at every point","the second derivative alone, with no value and no slope"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The approximation keeps the value and multiplies the derivative at the chosen point by the step. The value at the new point is what the approximation is trying to estimate, so it cannot be an ingredient. A ratio of value to coordinate is a different number from the slope. The second derivative is a later correction, not a replacement for the value and the slope.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 176\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>A smooth curve</title><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"318\" y2=\"146\"/><polygon points=\"318,146 311.6,148.7 311.6,143.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"24\"/><polygon points=\"46,24 48.7,30.4 43.3,30.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">y</text><polyline points=\"46,132.9 49,132.9 52,132.9 55.1,132.9 58.1,132.9 61.1,132.8 64.1,132.8 67.2,132.8 70.2,132.8 73.2,132.8 76.2,132.7 79.2,132.7 82.3,132.6 85.3,132.6 88.3,132.5 91.3,132.4 94.4,132.3 97.4,132.2 100.4,132.1 103.4,132 106.4,131.8 109.5,131.7 112.5,131.5 115.5,131.3 118.5,131.1 121.6,130.9 124.6,130.6 127.6,130.3 130.6,130 133.6,129.7 136.7,129.4 139.7,129 142.7,128.6 145.7,128.2 148.8,127.8 151.8,127.3 154.8,126.9 157.8,126.3 160.8,125.8 163.9,125.2 166.9,124.6 169.9,124 172.9,123.3 176,122.6 179,121.9 182,121.1 185,120.3 188,119.5 191.1,118.6 194.1,117.7 197.1,116.8 200.1,115.8 203.2,114.8 206.2,113.7 209.2,112.6 212.2,111.4 215.2,110.3 218.3,109 221.3,107.7 224.3,106.4 227.3,105.1 230.4,103.6 233.4,102.2 236.4,100.7 239.4,99.1 242.4,97.5 245.5,95.9 248.5,94.1 251.5,92.4 254.5,90.6 257.6,88.7 260.6,86.8 263.6,84.8 266.6,82.8 269.6,80.7 272.7,78.6 275.7,76.4 278.7,74.1 281.7,71.8 284.8,69.4 287.8,67 290.8,64.4 293.8,61.9 296.8,59.3 299.9,56.6 302.9,53.8 305.9,51 308.9,48.1 312,45.1 315,42.1 318,39\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A smooth curve that bends as its input grows."},{"id":"local-linear-approximation-r1-q2","version":1,"round":1,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Let \\(f(t) = t^3\\). At \\(t = 2\\), with a step of \\(0.1\\), what value does the linear approximation give?","answer":9.2,"unit":"","tolerance":0.02,"explanation":"At \\(t = 2\\) the value is \\(8\\) and the derivative \\(3t^2\\) is \\(12\\). The approximation is \\(8 + 12 \\cdot 0.1 = 9.2\\). Using the derivative at the new point \\(2.1\\), or reporting only the slope \\(12\\), answers a different question. The step is added only after it is multiplied by the slope.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 176\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>A smooth curve</title><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"318\" y2=\"146\"/><polygon points=\"318,146 311.6,148.7 311.6,143.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"24\"/><polygon points=\"46,24 48.7,30.4 43.3,30.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"star